{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 37, "model_name": "mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct-v0.1", "content": " Raymond III of Tripoli was a Count of Tripoli in the late 12th century Born in 1140 Raymond III was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Tripoli Raymond III inherited the County of Tripoli after his father's death in 1152 when he was just 12 years old During his minority the county was ruled by regents including his mother and the Knights Templar\n\n*Early reign and the Battle of Artah*\n\nRaymond III's reign was marked by conflict with Nur ad-Din the Zengid ruler of Aleppo and Damascus In 1157 Nur ad-Din launched an attack on the County of Tripoli and defeated Raymond III at the Battle of Artah Raymond III was forced to pay tribute to Nur ad-Din and give up some of his territories\n\n*Crusade of 1164 and the Battle of Harim*\n\nIn 1164 Raymond III joined the Crusade of 1164 led by King Amalric of Jerusalem The Crusaders aimed to capture Egypt but were defeated by Nur ad-Din at the Battle of Harim Raymond III was captured and held prisoner for 11 years in Aleppo\n\n*Release and later reign*\n\nRaymond III was released in 1175 after the intervention of the Byzantine Emperor Manuel I Comnenus Upon his release Raymond III returned to Tripoli and continued to rule the county until his death in 1187 Raymond III was succeeded by his son Bohemond III\n\nRaymond III's reign was marked by conflict with Muslim rulers and his relationship with the Knights Templar Despite these challenges Raymond III managed to maintain the County of Tripoli as a strong and prosperous state.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -3.9219088648678735e-05}, {"token": "III", "logprob": -0.0001627074379939586}, {"token": "of", "logprob": -1.2544739246368408}, {"token": "Tri", "logprob": -2.455681169521995e-05}, {"token": "pol", "logprob": -1.7881377516459906e-06}, {"token": "i", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "was", "logprob": -0.07846320420503616}, {